WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.740
A team day should feel like one event.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:06.380
Northstar Studio is bringing its new Beacon team together.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.060
Maya plans a welcome, a quick warm-up, two working

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.190
discussions and time to build.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.040
People join once.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.040
The host guides them through the day.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:19.059
Maya gives each item a title, a length, and

00:00:19.069 --> 00:00:20.129
a presenter.

00:00:20.179 --> 00:00:25.589
Presentations, trivia, call and answer, surveys and offline activities

00:00:25.589 --> 00:00:27.719
can sit in the same agenda.

00:00:27.809 --> 00:00:29.539
Breaks have a place too.

00:00:29.749 --> 00:00:32.499
The plan gives the team a clear order without

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:34.891
forcing every activity onto a screen.

00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:36.941
The welcome sets the scene.

00:00:36.991 --> 00:00:38.921
Beacon is still an idea.

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:41.341
The team needs to learn who it should help,

00:00:41.391 --> 00:00:44.501
which problem matters, and what to test first.

00:00:44.551 --> 00:00:48.041
Maya uses a short presentation to frame the day.

00:00:48.231 --> 00:00:51.446
Participants can see which agenda item is live.

00:00:51.496 --> 00:00:54.936
Next, Maya starts the 80s trivia warm-up.

00:00:54.976 --> 00:00:57.986
Priya stays in the same event, and her screen

00:00:57.986 --> 00:01:00.006
follows the host into the game.

00:01:00.236 --> 00:01:02.716
She answers a quick music question.

00:01:02.766 --> 00:01:05.196
It gives the team a light start before they

00:01:05.196 --> 00:01:08.065
turn to the harder questions about their new project.

00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:10.355
Maya opens Direction Builder.

00:01:10.415 --> 00:01:12.515
The team moves from a quick answer to a

00:01:12.665 --> 00:01:13.835
thoughtful one.

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:17.795
Priya suggests starting with small software product teams and

00:01:17.795 --> 00:01:21.315
asking about the last customer request they lost.

00:01:21.355 --> 00:01:24.175
A laptop or tablet works best here.

00:01:24.235 --> 00:01:27.414
People need room to write, read, and respond.

00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:29.844
A break is part of the event, too.

00:01:29.924 --> 00:01:31.844
Maya starts it from the agenda.

00:01:32.204 --> 00:01:34.884
Priya sees the break and a return time on

00:01:34.884 --> 00:01:35.964
her screen.

00:01:36.044 --> 00:01:38.724
No one needs a new link or code.

00:01:38.804 --> 00:01:41.394
When the team is ready, the host chooses the

00:01:41.394 --> 00:01:44.161
next engagement and brings everyone back together.

00:01:44.261 --> 00:01:47.891
For new ideas, Maya starts lessons from the School

00:01:47.891 --> 00:01:49.021
of Hard Books.

00:01:49.021 --> 00:01:51.671
A lesson from medicine asks the team to do

00:01:51.751 --> 00:01:52.791
no harm.

00:01:52.881 --> 00:01:57.131
Priya proposes trying sample data before changing a customer's

00:01:57.131 --> 00:01:57.771
tools.

00:01:57.901 --> 00:02:00.751
The same event can make room for a warmup,

00:02:00.751 --> 00:02:03.702
a focused discussion, and a fresh way to think.

00:02:03.762 --> 00:02:06.372
The rest of the agenda leaves time for prototype

00:02:06.482 --> 00:02:09.592
planning, meeting feedback and next steps.

00:02:09.642 --> 00:02:12.552
Maya decides what goes live and when.

00:02:12.602 --> 00:02:15.392
Throughout the day, the team keeps the same event

00:02:15.392 --> 00:02:16.632
code, 9630.

00:02:16.632 --> 00:02:21.692
Plan the flow, invite your people, and guide the

00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:23.286
next useful conversation.
